---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [{
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: 'Can I share business logic between an iOS app and an Android app with Traverse?',
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'Yes at the package level, with pre-1.0 caveats. The same capability and contract run through the Swift embedder (a wasmi-based host distributed as an XCFramework) on iOS and the Kotlin embedder (Chicory, published on Maven Central at 0.14.0) on Android, and both have reference clients. Neither is Certified under Spec 529 yet, and both are still marked In progress on the Platforms page.',
    },
  }],
});

const relatedLinks = [
  { href: '/questions/can-traverse-build-mobile-apps.html', label: 'Can I build a mobile app with Traverse?' },
  { href: '/blog/native-ios-runtime-foundation.html', label: 'Native iOS without a JIT: a hands-on feasibility proof' },
  { href: '/platforms.html', label: 'Where Traverse actually runs (platform status)' },
  { href: '/questions/what-framework-runs-business-logic-on-any-os.html', label: 'What framework lets me write business logic once and run it on any OS?' },
];
---
<QuestionLayout
  title="Can I share business logic between an iOS app and an Android app with Traverse?"
  description="Yes at the package level, with pre-1.0 caveats: the Swift embedder on iOS and the Kotlin embedder on Android (Maven Central 0.14.0) run the same capability. Neither host is Certified yet."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/can-i-share-logic-between-ios-and-android-apps.html"
  category="Question"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>This is exactly the problem Traverse is built to solve for mobile, and the packages to do it now exist on both sides. It's still worth being precise about how mature each side is, because "installable" and "certified" are different claims.</p>

  <p>On iOS, <code>packages/swift/TraverseEmbedder</code> now ships a production runtime bridge: <code>WasmiHostBridgeClient</code> embeds the <code>wasmi</code> interpreter behind a narrow C ABI, packaged as the <code>TraverseSwiftHost</code> XCFramework, with host-enforced memory and fuel limits. Its resource controls follow Spec 074, governed by ADR-0014 and ADR-0015. The earlier WasmKit-based bridge has been removed from the package; WasmKit is not supported.</p>

  <p>On Android, <code>packages/kotlin/TraverseEmbedder</code> has a working runtime bridge built on Chicory, published on Maven Central as <code>com.traverse-framework:traverse-embedder</code> (<code>0.14.0</code>). The iOS and Android clients in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/reference-apps" target="_blank" rel="noopener">reference-apps</a> run the runtime in-process, with no sidecar server.</p>

  <p>What's not done: both hosts are pre-1.0, and Spec 529's host-neutral Certified/Preview classification needs a five-platform conformance runner that's later work, so no host is Certified today.</p>

  <h2>What you can do about it now</h2>
  <p>Write the shared rule as one well-scoped Traverse capability and embed it on both phones, keeping UI native on each side. Pin exact package versions, test on both hosts, and watch <a href="/platforms.html">Platforms</a> for status changes rather than guessing from a roadmap date. If you need a certified, post-1.0 host today, that isn't here yet.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
